  <server name="cgis">
    Use tools from this server through `execute` under `tools["cgis"]`.
    cgis holds a deterministic graph of a codebase: every function, method and class, and the resolved calls, imports, inheritance and references between them. Use it for questions about how code connects, before grepping across files or reading whole modules for context.

    When to use which tool:
    - Unfamiliar repository, where to start: cgis_overview.
    - What calls X / is changing or renaming X safe: cgis_analyze_impact.
    - What does X end up calling: cgis_trace_flow.
    - How a module or class is laid out: cgis_get_structure.
    - How one package is built inside, module by module: cgis_module_graph.
    - One symbol with its source and neighbours, before editing it: cgis_context.
    - Where is the symbol named X: cgis_find_symbol.
    - Coupling, drift, reachability of a guard: cgis_metrics, cgis_drift, cgis_audit_reachability.
    - Before review or merge, what else a diff may have broken: cgis_diff_check.

    First run: if a tool answers "Database not found", build the graph with cgis_ingest(project_path=<source root>) — the source root, not the repository root, so virtualenvs and node_modules stay out. Prefer an absolute db_path: a relative one resolves against the server's working directory, not yours.

    Trust the answer for what it covers; do not re-grep to confirm a resolved edge. Read it honestly:
    - freshness: when files changed since the last ingest, or other cgis code built the graph, a graph answer says so — a freshness key in json, a note above a diagram (cgis_find_symbol carries none). A stale graph answers confidently and wrongly — re-run cgis_ingest (incremental) before relying on it.
    - coverage / top_unresolved (json): calls on values whose type is decided at runtime (an injected client, logger.info) resolve to nothing. An empty impact result means "no static callers found", not "safe to delete": dispatch through a registry, a decorator or a framework is invisible. In Python, a function handed to map, sorted(key=), threading.Thread(target=), an executor or the event loop, or to a project function that calls that parameter, has an edge from the code that hands it over, as does one held in a local and called; one stored for a framework to call has no caller. Each unresolved name comes with where it is made ("at <caller> <file:line>" in text, coverage.sites in json). When one could reach the symbol you asked about (impact) or lead past the answer (flow), read that line and follow it before answering; naming the gap is not enough.
    - cgis_analyze_impact and cgis_trace_flow leave out test code (counted) and cap the answer at max_nodes, nearest first; a cut is reported. Ask for output_format="text" to read an answer — an indented tree, a fifth the size of json; use json to compute with it.
    - cgis_diff_check: caller and subclass items are leads to read; data-key items are noisy — measured on one application (owner-api), about a quarter of listed items were worth a look (docs/lab-notes/2026-10-19-data-key-diff-check.md). Before judging a code site that reads or writes a key unaffected, read its callers (cgis_analyze_impact): they decide what values reach it. Key items need a graph ingested with data keys on (cgis_ingest(data_keys=True)); without them the answer says so.

    Coverage: Python (.py), TypeScript (.ts, .tsx) and Go (.go) only; JSON and YAML files (.json, .yaml, .yml) contribute data-key declarations only when data keys are enabled (`cgis_ingest(data_keys=True)`, CLI `--data-keys`). Other languages are not in the graph — read those files, and say which part of the answer the graph could not see.
    Go is supported for structure and metrics only. Go callers are a lead, not a list: confirm them by reading the code. A function value held in a local and called through it has an edge (20 of 20 held-out calls, cgis #695). One passed to another function that calls it has an edge only if that function is on cgis's invoker list (sync.Once.Do, time.AfterFunc, sort.Slice and similar ones are); this is barely measured. A callback stored for a framework to call (a handler, cobra's RunE) has no caller: treat it as an entry point. Some interface dispatch is missing (exp4: measured on one library, golang/crypto, mostly its ssh package; not measured on applications).
    JSON and YAML files contribute data key declarations only: no symbols, calls or impact. An OpenSearch mapping declares each property and each subfield under its fields (status.keyword is declared only by a keyword subfield).
  </server>
  <server name="context7">
    Use tools from this server through `execute` under `tools["context7"]`.
    Use this server to fetch current documentation whenever the user asks about a library, framework, SDK, API, CLI tool, or cloud service — even well-known ones like React, Next.js, Prisma, Express, Tailwind, Django, or Spring Boot. This includes API syntax, configuration, version migration, library-specific debugging, setup instructions, and CLI tool usage. Use even when you think you know the answer — your training data may not reflect recent changes. Prefer this over web search for library docs.

    Do not use for: refactoring, writing scripts from scratch, debugging business logic, code review, or general programming concepts.
  </server>
  <server name="github">
    Use tools from this server through `execute` under `tools["github"]`.
    The GitHub MCP Server provides tools to interact with GitHub platform.

    Tool selection guidance:
    	1. Use 'list_*' tools for broad, simple retrieval and pagination of all items of a type (e.g., all issues, all PRs, all branches) with basic filtering.
    	2. Use 'search_*' tools for targeted queries with specific criteria, keywords, or complex filters (e.g., issues with certain text, PRs by author, code containing functions).

    Context management:
    	1. Use pagination whenever possible with batches of 5-10 items.
    	2. Use minimal_output parameter set to true if the full information is not needed to accomplish a task.

    Tool usage guidance:
    	1. For 'search_*' tools: Use separate 'sort' and 'order' parameters if available for sorting results - do not include 'sort:' syntax in query strings. Query strings should contain only search criteria (e.g., 'org:google language:python'), not sorting instructions. Always call 'get_me' first to understand current user permissions and context. ## Issues

    Check 'list_issue_types' first for organizations to use proper issue types. Use 'search_issues' before creating new issues to avoid duplicates. Always set 'state_reason' when closing issues. ## Pull Requests

    PR review workflow: Always use 'pull_request_review_write' with method 'create' to create a pending review, then 'add_comment_to_pending_review' to add comments, and finally 'pull_request_review_write' with method 'submit_pending' to submit the review for complex reviews with line-specific comments.

    Before creating a pull request, search for pull request templates in the repository. Template files are called pull_request_template.md or they're located in '.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE' directory. Use the template content to structure the PR description and then call create_pull_request tool.
  </server>
